layout: base.njk
title: "Insights"
summary: "Articles and updates from Natural Trace on molecular traceability, supply chain authenticity, and the natural products industry."
permalink: "/insights/"
---
{#-
  One page, one row per article.

  Modelled on channelnewsasia.com/topic/insight, which Bryan asked for on
  1 September 2026 after the card layout was measured at roughly 700px per
  article. CNA's row pitch is 136px: a small picture, a category kicker, the
  headline, a date, and no summary. That is about five times denser than the
  cards this replaces.

  The whole archive fits on one page at that density. Twenty articles come to
  about 2,700px of scroll, against the 6,300px one of the three paginated
  pages used to cost, so the pager is gone rather than resized. Every article
  the company has published is now reachable without a click.

  Revisit the pager at roughly fifty articles. Nothing here is load-bearing for
  it: restoring pagination is putting the front matter back, and the wording
  and the numbers are in docs/rollback-v3.md.

  What was deliberately not taken from CNA. The sort control, the content-type
  filters, the category facet and the follow button are all answers to having
  166 items and a daily audience. At twenty items and a reader who visits when
  something prompts them, every one of those is furniture for a problem this
  page does not have, and an empty filter is worse than no filter.

  The summary is the real cost and it is a content decision rather than a
  layout one. A news headline sells itself; "Beyond Botanical Identity" tells a
  supplement buyer very little on its own. The summary field is still written,
  still required, and still doing three other jobs: the meta description, the
  Article schema description, and the body of the LinkedIn post the Zapier flow
  builds. It is only absent from this index.
-#}

<section class="insights-listing">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="insights-header">
      <span class="section-label">Insights</span>
      <h1>News &amp; Updates</h1>
      <p class="insights-subtitle">The latest from Natural Trace: product developments, industry perspectives and company news.</p>
    </div>

    {% set total = collections.insightsRegular.length + collections.insightsPinned.length %}
    {% if total > 0 %}
    {#- The count is CNA's "Showing 15 of 166" without the first half, because
        nothing is being withheld: everything is on this page, so a "showing"
        would be answering a question nobody is left asking. It is here to say
        how big the archive is before a reader starts scrolling it. -#}
    <p class="insights-count">{{ total }} article{% if total != 1 %}s{% endif %}</p>

    <div class="insights-rows">
      {% for post in collections.insightsRegular %}
        {% include "partials/insight-row.njk" %}
      {% endfor %}

      {#- The evergreen introduction, last. It was appended to the final
          paginated page for the same reason: an introduction to an archive
          belongs at the end of it, not above this month's news. With one page
          that placement stops being a pagination trick and becomes simply
          where it sits. -#}
      {% for post in collections.insightsPinned %}
        {% include "partials/insight-row.njk" %}
      {% endfor %}
    </div>

    {% else %}
    <div class="insights-empty">
      <h2>Coming Soon</h2>
      <p>We're preparing our first insights. Check back soon for news, updates, and industry perspectives from the Natural Trace team.</p>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
</section>

{#- Posts that were only ever going to be LinkedIn posts.
    Content comes from src/_data/linkedin.yml, edited under "From our LinkedIn"
    in the CMS. Nothing is fetched from LinkedIn at build time: an editor
    chooses a post and pastes its address. LinkedIn publishes no feed of a
    company page's posts, and the API that would answer for one needs an app
    review and a key that expires every sixty days, which is not a thing to put
    in a build that four people share.

    Below the articles, not above them. The articles are the version of record
    (docs/publishing-to-linkedin.md sets out why) and this is an aside to them.

    The first-page condition this used to carry is gone with the pager. There
    is only one page now, so there is no last page for it to land on by
    accident.

    A button rather than the post itself, which is the part most likely to look
    like an oversight later. Loading the frame loads a LinkedIn page, and that
    sets LinkedIn's cookies for every visitor who reaches this page, whatever
    they answered in the consent bar. The bar covers our own analytics and says
    so; stretching that answer to cover somebody else's tracking would make the
    wording untrue. So the frame waits to be asked, and the asking is the
    consent. With JavaScript off, or if main.js fails to load, the link below
    the button still goes to the post. -#}
{% if linkedin.posts and linkedin.posts.length > 0 %}
<section class="linkedin-strip">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="insights-header">
      <span class="section-label">LinkedIn</span>
      <h2>{{ linkedin.heading }}</h2>
      {% if linkedin.subheading %}<p class="insights-subtitle">{{ linkedin.subheading }}</p>{% endif %}
    </div>

    <div class="linkedin-grid">
      {% for post in linkedin.posts %}
      <div class="linkedin-embed"
           data-embed-src="{{ post.url | linkedinEmbed }}"
           data-embed-title="LinkedIn post: {{ post.title }}">
        <p class="linkedin-embed-label">{{ post.title }}</p>
        <button type="button" class="linkedin-embed-load">Show this post</button>
        <p class="linkedin-embed-note">
          Loads the post from LinkedIn, which sets its own cookies.
          <a href="{{ post.url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open it on LinkedIn instead</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
{% endif %}
